re many many many ways that  the tumor microenvironment can cause T  cells to become dysfunctional and  there's so many I don't have time to get  into all of them today there are some  really awesome reviews out there that  you can read if you'd like to learn more  about driving to cell  function in the micro environment but we  knew that all of these were  possibilities any one of these or any  combination of these could be the reason  that our T cells are not retaining  function and and not persisting in the  tumor microenvironment so we wanted to  ask which features of this mouse model  we were using were similar to the  features that were present in human  ovarian cancer that way we could better  understand which obstacles we could  actually study in the Mouse disease so  we wanted to understand how much these  tumors in the id8 veg f Mouse model  actually recapitulated features of human  disease and to do this we use thermal  fissures applied Biosystems deep  transcriptome profiling in order to  truly understand what genes are being  overexpressed and what pathways are the  same or different between our  preclinical model and real patient  disease so to do this we got Mouse  samples from our tumor bearing mice at  late stages of disease and we were able  to get from a local repository some  samples of human disease from patients  and these samples that we obtained were  matched samples so we have samples from  five patients we have their primary  tumor we also have a sample of  metastatic disease from the same person  and we also have a matched normal tissue  sample or adjacent normal from the same  person that way we can really start to  ask questions about does the mouse model  look more like primary disease does it  look more like metastatic disease and  having samples from the same matched  patient actually makes the analysis nice  and clean so we got samples that were  fresh frozen yet surgery so either at  Mouse euthanasia and necropsy or in the  clinic when the patient was undergoing  debulking surgery we did RNA extraction  on all of these samples ourselves and  did quality testing to see what the  quality level was and in the vast  majority of cases we had really good  quality RNA but there were a few samples  that came back with slightly lower Rin  values and so when we sent our samples  to the thermal Fisher team they actually  were able to do quality  control and checks on their end and told  us that even though the Rinne values and  the quality was a little lower that they  thought the asset would still  potentially work and we were fortunate  that it did so we sent our samples to  the services labs and they performed the  applied Biosystems microarray for us and  what was really nice about this we had  them do the clarium deep eco assay and  we chose the Pico assay because we knew  that some of the RNA samples we had from  our particular tissues we're quite  limited in quantity and so we wanted to  do something that was incredibly good at  low low levels of input and then Clary  MD is one of the more recent microarray  designs and what's really nice about  this one is that the probes actually  cover and they have great coverage of  all the exons that you would care about  but they also cover splicing sites so  you can get a lot of information about  different splice of isoforms an  expression of non-coding RNAs as well so  then for software analysis we utilized  both the transcriptome analysis console  the tax software that comes for free  with with the assay and we use that to  look a little bit at what our tumors  actually looked like relative to normal  samples and we also used it to look at  just gene expression overall what genes  were up or down in these tumors relative  to normal tissue and then we also used  our not only to validate those findings  but also to look at how similar and how  different the mouse and human samples  were to one another of tumors so first I  wanted to show you a sample of what the  taksaka actually looks like so this is  just a screenshot of one of my analyses  this is looking at the different human  samples that we had run and this is  simply showing a gene expression of a  handful of genes from this particular  array and all of the different ways that  you can analyze this data so the QC view  and summary of you are really great  because they give you sort of an  overview of the quality of your samples  and all of the different data that you  got back the gene view lets you dive  much deeper into all the p-values the  FDR p-values and figure out which genes  are actually over  expressed or downregulated in each  individual sample and then one of the  other really cool things that this  program or this analysis lets you look  at is the alternative splicing so I'm  not going to talk about that today  because we didn't use it for this  particular question but it's one more  really cool powerful use of this  particular assay so in addition you can  take this gene expression information  and you can plot it in heat maps and  compare your samples and truly see where  the patterns are and you can export that  and use it for figures if you need to  and again one of the great things about  the software that we really appreciated  was that you could measure so many  different things even things that you  might not have thought to ask about so  we were able to look at gene expression  changes which was our major question but  we were also if we wanted able to  evaluate and dig down into splice  variants and non-coding RNAs and that  coverage especially of those lowly  abundant low abundant transcripts was  really helpful for our particular  analysis so one of the main concern I  had and wanted to sort of evaluate  before we went too far was whether or  not the human samples that we had gotten  from the repository looked like  high-grade serous ovarian cancer so high  grade serous is just one of many  subtypes of ovarian cancer and that's  the 