immunotherapy now that said there  definitely were some pathways that were  not the same and most of these came out  as metabolic pathways which isn't  completely surprising we know that this  is a tumor cell line that is grown and  maintained in vitro so tumor cells again  adapt very readily to their environment  and so it's not surprising that these  cells have over expression of lots of  metabolic pathways it just means that  when we truly are evaluating our data  from these mice we'll have to keep that  in mind that the metabolic environment  in these tumors is much harsher than  what we will probably be up against in  patient tumors so going forward we found  all these pathways that were very very  similar and several packets that were  different but ultimately because most of  the pathways that were similar were the  ones that were incredibly relevant or  things like suppressive cells or antigen  processing in presentation we decided  this seemed like a good model to keep  moving forward with in terms of  evaluating whether or not adoptive  t-cell therapy with engineered cell  could prolong survival in these advanced  stage mice so we did go back and try to  validate some of these immunosuppressive  features using immunohistochemistry so  here I'm showing for example staining  for myeloid cells which are very  well-known immunosuppressive cell in  tumor microenvironment  so this is data both from human  high-grade serous samples as well as the  mouse id8 veg a tumor these are tumors  again that were grown in these mice for  at least eight to ten weeks before we  took these samples and we've seen  similar data for regulatory t-cells as  well the suppressive cells are  definitely present in both systems and  we've also seen that both human and  mouse disease overexpress or at least  have abundant expression of the I gather  proposed for some of those inhibitory  receptors that I showed you before are  present on our t-cells when we pull them  out after they've been in tumors for  about 21 plus days so for example  collected 9 has been proposed as one of  the ligand sorts in 3 and collectin 3 is  one of the ligands proposed fine to lag  3 which both send negative signals to  t-cells so armed with this and armed  with some preliminary data that  suggested that a single dose of t-cells  did not prolong survival at all we  decided to go at this really in and hit  it as hard as we could so we made our  t-cells and this time instead of just  injecting one dose we decided well these  t-cells are becoming dysfunctional  fairly quickly so let's give fresh  t-cells Oh often let's give fresh  t-cells every 14 days so that we're  constantly hitting the tumours with  effector T cells that have the potential  to kill them and in addition to just  being t-cells we're gonna give T cells  and boost those T cells in vivo so this  is actually a protocol that was  pioneered in a cancer cell paper by one  of my colleagues and Vince promised in  2015 and so we essentially recapitulated  or our utilized that treatment technique  in these mice to see if it would prolong  survival so again we wait until there's  detectable disease at a late stage and  then we give a limpid depletion regimen  to simulate what often happens in the  clinic  this has also been shown in the  literature to promote self persistence  donor self persistence  so we tried treating with cytoxan to  Limbo to flee prior to treat t-cell  treatment and then and when we give  t-cells this time we give T cells with  peptide post irradiated splenocytes that  way we're giving those T cells a quick  antigen boost in vivo to try to expand  them and increase those numbers in the  animal so we only give the limpo Dupree  lympho depletion regimen once for the  first treatment after that we give fresh  T cells and this vaccine boost every 14  days and we followed the mice to look at  how long they would survive and in this  case I will note that we are giving Isle  to every time we give T 