 I teamed up with a  colleague of mine in the lab dr. Shannon  Oda who's an excellent molecular  molecular biologist and she engineered  this panel of what we're calling  immunomodulators fusion proteins so  there's an ecto domain that is the fast  receptor so this is where the molecule  actually will see or recognize that fast  ligand signal but instead of having the  intracellular domains that would send a  death signal she used it with molecules  that will send a positive signal  something that will either some support  proliferation or survival to try to act  both as a dominant negative so that the  T cells are ignoring or not receiving  that death signal but also to override  it and send even more of a positive  supportive signal to the T cells so  essentially trying to use the tumor  evasion mechanism against itself so we  did several different versions of this  constructs as you can see on the screen  I'm just showing you a couple of  examples we actually tested a really  huge array of these molecules and we  narrowed it down to some that we thought  had the most potential and so here I'm  showing you cd28 and 401 B B which have  actually been talked about in the  literature before as switch signals or  ways that you can send a positive signal  to t-cells and we did a lot of in vitro  experiments to try to identify which of  these had the greatest potential in vivo  and for the sake of time I'm not going  to show you all of those experiments but  I'll tell you that it looked like based  on the preliminary experiments that we  did the fast or when BD constructs  looked much stronger so in terms of  cytokine production and the  proliferation and survival and  transcription factor expression all of  those were consistently better in  fast forward baby cells so we took that  into our mice and tried to add this to  the survival curve see how cells Express  both the mesothelioma dilatory fusion  protein could function in vivo and we  were really excited to see that when we  include one of these fastball and beanie  constructs we actually see even greater  prolonging of survival than if we simply  treat with needs of feelings he sells  alone now one construct worked much  better than the other one didn't seem to  do anything in vivo suggesting that that  molecular biology that you do to make  these constructs really matters quite a  bit but again we were able to overcome  an obstacle that we had identified in  the tumor microenvironment and  essentially figure out a way around it  and make our therapy better because we  made an informed set of experiments  mimicking something that is present in  the mouse model that we know is relevant  for human disease so in conclusion we've  covered a lot of ground today but I  wanted you to walk away knowing that  this idea id8 veg F model actually does  recapitulate many of the key features or  hallmarks of human high grade serous  ovarian cancer including the expression  of a lot of these immunosuppressive  features that are problematic and pose a  lot of limitations or hurdles for  immunotherapy of many kinds and then  also that if you identify an immuno  competent preclinical model that does  characterize or recapitulate features of  human disease  you have this ability to then design  therapeutic strategies that will get  around some of those and those  potentially have increased relevance for  translation in the clinic so with that I  want to thank all of the people that  contributed to this work I have a great  team here at the Fred hutch of  technicians and medical fellows and  undergrads that all work incredibly hard  to get these experiments done I also  want to thank my mentor Phil Greenberg  without whom none of this project just  wouldn't exist shannon oda dr. Shannon  Oda for all of her help  making these fusion proteins because  without her this last Avenue of the  projects wouldn't be what it is and then  again to all of the people who gave us  technologies and samples so that we  could do the really great work that  we're doing and obviously to the funding  sources because without them this work  wouldn't exist either so with that I am  happy to any questions that you have on  what we've done so far  thank you dr. Anderson for your  informative presentation we will now  start the live Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the answer question box located on  the far left of your screen well answer  as many of your questions if so you have  time for let's get started our first  question is is the target antigen a self  protein and did you see the toxicity  that's a great question and one that we  do get all the time so thank you very  much for bringing that up  so Musa Phelan is actually a self  protein and that's actually one of the  reasons that we chose to target it is  because an NCI working group selected  this as a really good kid or antigen  simply because it while it is expressed  in some healthy tissues it's actually  expressed in a very very low level and  in many many tumors it's expressed  sometimes ten or a hundreds of times  greater so that actually makes it a good  antigen because the chances of on target  off tumor toxicity are fairly low so  we've been keeping an eye on this  because as you might expect we are  suspicious that targeting a self protein  could cause toxicities or pathologies in  tumor or in excuse me tissues that we  don't want it to and fortunately in all  of the pathology that we have done all  of the basically heme slides that we  have sent all of the pathologists we've  talked to in multiple different  preclinical models we haven't seen any  toxicities so while we have seen  engineered t cells in tissues that have  mesothelioma expressed at normal healthy  levels we haven't seen any pathologies  or any mice that have succumbed to  pathological issues due to t cell  treatment  wonderful thank you our next question  says did Clarion provide you with enough  trim scriptum information for your  project yes actually the Clary MD assay  that we had performed provided us with  above and beyond what we actually needed  for our basic question so we when we got  our data back not only got data on gene  expression but we were also able to get  information on isoforms and splice  variants and things that we are going to  be mining for years to come because we  got so much data out of this one assay  our next question says how was your  experience with tax software for data  analysis how easy was it to learn and  use that's a great question so I am NOT  a bioinformatics expert my training was  in immunology as a graduate student and  then in cancer biology and molecular  biology as a postdoc and so I actually  leaned on thermo Fisher and the tax  software team to learn it the first time  around they were kind enough to send out  a rep to teach me for free how to use  the software to talk me through all of  the logistics and left me with lots of  notes and information that I could  basically teach myself so when I finally  sat down and worked through the software  on my own it was really intuitive and  actually very easy to use so I only  enlisted a bioinformatics expert to make  sure that I had done it right and was  happy to find out that I had so thank  you what kind of combination therapies  are most compatible with engineered T  cell therapy that's a great question and  one that we're still actively  investigating as I showed in the  presentation we think that further  engineering additional components of  cellular petition components of T cells  is very powerful but we think that this  could actually be combined with many  different types of techniques for  example combining this with checkpoint  blockade something that's already in the  clinic has great potential for making  these cells more functional these tumors  along that I currently are  all right we have time for one more  question and this one